  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified chart of the Standard Model, organizing elementary particles into two broad groups: fermions and bosons. Fermions, shown on the left, are the matter particles and are divided into quarks and leptons. Each of these is arranged into three generations, labeled first, second, and third, suggesting a repeating family structure. The quarks are paired as up/down, charm/strange, and top/bottom, while the leptons are electron/electron neutrino, muon/muon neutrino, and tau/tau neutrino.\n\nOn the right, the bosons are shown as force-related particles. Most are grouped as gauge bosons: the photon, gluon, W boson, and Z boson. Beneath them, the Higgs boson is shown separately, indicating a distinct role from the gauge bosons.\n\nVisually, the main message is the classification of known fundamental particles by type and family. The layout emphasizes the contrast between matter particles and force carriers, while also highlighting the three-generation pattern among quarks and leptons. The color coding and boxed grouping make the hierarchy clear and easy to compare at a glance.",
